-- Q1: easy / L1 — Simple vs complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["A complex structure has more than one class of common stock", "A complex structure reports income from discontinued operations", "A complex structure includes securities that could potentially dilute EPS", "A complex structure has preferred stock outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15, a complex capital structure includes outstanding securities or agreements that could potentially dilute EPS, such as convertible bonds, stock options, or warrants. A simple capital structure has only common stock with no potentially dilutive securities. Having preferred stock alone does not create a complex structure unless the preferred stock is convertible into common shares.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "Public entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market", "Any entity with more than $10 million in total revenue", "All entities with outstanding preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, only public entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market must present EPS. Private companies are not required to report EPS, though they may voluntarily do so. An entity''s size or revenue level does not determine the EPS reporting requirement — it depends solely on whether the entity''s securities are publicly traded.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted method calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glendale Industries reports net income of $720,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. It has 5,000 shares of $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each convertible into 5 common shares. What is diluted EPS using the if-converted method?', '["$2.88", "$3.40", "$3.02", "$3.20"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back preferred dividends ($40,000) to the numerator and adds converted shares (5,000 x 5 = 25,000) to the denominator. Diluted EPS = $720,000 / (200,000 + 25,000) = $3.20. This is less than basic EPS of $3.40, confirming dilution. Choice C ($3.02) incorrectly deducts the preferred dividends and adds the shares, double-penalizing common shareholders.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Mid-year actual conversion effect on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Corp. had 10,000 shares of convertible preferred stock outstanding on January 1, each convertible into 3 common shares. On July 1, all preferred shares were converted. The annual preferred dividend is $50,000. Analyze the effect of this mid-year conversion on the numerator and denominator of basic EPS for the year.', '["Deduct $25,000 preferred dividends from the numerator; add 30,000 common shares weighted for half the year to the denominator", "Deduct $50,000 preferred dividends from the numerator; add 30,000 common shares for the full year to the denominator", "No preferred dividend deduction because the shares were converted; add 30,000 common shares weighted for half the year to the denominator", "Deduct $25,000 preferred dividends from the numerator; add 30,000 common shares for the full year to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-12, when convertible preferred stock is actually converted during the period, basic EPS deducts preferred dividends only for the pre-conversion period ($50,000 x 6/12 = $25,000) and includes the new common shares in the weighted average only from the conversion date (30,000 shares x 6/12). Choice C is incorrect because dividends for the pre-conversion period must still be deducted from the numerator.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Control number: loss from continuing ops with discontinued ops income
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Holdings reports a loss from continuing operations of $180,000 and income from discontinued operations of $250,000, resulting in net income of $70,000. The company has 50,000 common shares and 5,000 dilutive stock options. The controller proposes reporting diluted EPS using the net income figure. Evaluate whether this approach is correct under ASC 260.', '["The approach is correct because net income is positive, so dilutive securities should be included", "The approach is incorrect because diluted EPS should use income from discontinued operations as the control number", "The approach is incorrect because the control number is the loss from continuing operations, making all potentially dilutive securities antidilutive", "The approach is correct because positive net income always requires inclusion of dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19A, the control number for determining whether potential common shares are dilutive is income from continuing operations (adjusted for preferred dividends). Because Westbrook reports a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and must be excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A incorrectly uses net income as the control number, which would overstate dilution contrary to ASC 260.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Definition of potential common shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what are potential common shares?', '["Securities or contracts that may entitle the holder to obtain common stock", "Shares of common stock authorized but not yet issued by the entity", "Treasury shares held by the company for future reissuance", "Shares reserved for employee bonus plans but not yet granted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, potential common shares are securities or other contracts that may entitle the holder to obtain common stock during or after the reporting period, such as convertible bonds, stock options, and warrants. Choice B is incorrect because authorized but unissued shares are not potential common shares — they have no contractual right attached that could result in dilution.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Reverse stock split retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how does a reverse stock split affect the computation of weighted-average shares?', '["The reverse split is applied prospectively from the effective date only", "The reverse split is applied retroactively to all periods presented", "The reverse split affects only diluted EPS, not basic EPS", "The reverse split is ignored for EPS purposes until the next fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits and reverse stock splits are given retroactive treatment in the EPS computation for all periods presented, as if the split occurred at the beginning of the earliest period. This ensures comparability across periods. Choice A is incorrect because prospective treatment would distort period-over-period comparisons and would not reflect the changed share structure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Required line items for EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which income statement line items must a public entity present per-share amounts?', '["Gross profit and net income only", "Operating income and net income only", "Net income only", "Income from continuing operations and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a public entity must present both basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and for net income on the face of the income statement. Per-share amounts for discontinued operations may be presented on the face or in the notes. Choice C is incomplete because it omits the required presentation for income from continuing operations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Treasury stock repurchase mid-year weighted average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On May 1, Meridian repurchased 60,000 shares as treasury stock. On November 1, Meridian reissued 30,000 of those treasury shares. What is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for the year?', '["260,000", "265,000", "270,000", "255,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares are computed based on the time each share count is outstanding. Meridian had 300,000 shares for 4 months (100,000), 240,000 shares for 6 months (120,000), and 270,000 shares for 2 months (45,000), totaling 265,000. Choice A (260,000) incorrectly weights the reissuance period, failing to properly account for the November 1 reissuance of treasury shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Multi-security diluted EPS sequencing (full calc)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hargrove Technologies reports net income of $900,000 with 200,000 weighted-average shares and $60,000 in preferred dividends (basic EPS = $4.20). The company has: (1) 10,000 stock options with a $30 exercise price (average market price $50), and (2) $500,000 of 8% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%) convertible into 25,000 shares. Evaluate the proper sequence for computing diluted EPS and determine the final amount.', '["$4.12, including only the stock options", "$3.84, including both securities but using pre-tax interest", "$3.67, including both securities but omitting the interest add-back", "$3.80, including both securities with proper after-tax interest adjustment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, dilutive securities are ranked by per-share impact: options ($0/4,000 incremental shares) first, then bonds ($30,000 after-tax interest / 25,000 shares = $1.20). Including options yields $840,000/204,000 = $4.12. Including bonds yields ($840,000 + $30,000)/(204,000 + 25,000) = $3.80. Both are dilutive. Choice C ($3.67) incorrectly omits the after-tax interest add-back required by the if-converted method.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Mid-year convertible bond interest add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, Ashland Corp. issues $600,000 of 6% convertible bonds at par, convertible into 15,000 common shares. Net income for the year is $450,000 with 100,000 weighted-average common shares and no preferred dividends. The tax rate is 25%. What interest adjustment is added to the numerator when computing diluted EPS for these bonds?', '["$27,000", "$18,000", "$13,500", "$36,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, when convertible bonds are outstanding for only part of the period, the if-converted method adds back only the after-tax interest for the period the bonds were outstanding. Ashland''s bonds were outstanding for 6 months: $600,000 x 6% x 6/12 x (1 - 25%) = $13,500. Choice B ($18,000) incorrectly uses pre-tax interest, failing to account for the tax deductibility of interest expense.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — RSUs in diluted EPS (unrecognized compensation cost)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinnacle Corp. granted 8,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) to employees on January 1 with a three-year vesting period. The average unrecognized compensation cost during the year was $120,000, and the average market price of Pinnacle''s stock was $40 per share. Under the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares result from the RSUs for diluted EPS?', '["5,000 shares", "8,000 shares", "3,000 shares", "4,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, nonvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds equal the average unrecognized compensation cost ($120,000). At Pinnacle''s $40 average market price, 3,000 shares ($120,000 / $40) are assumed repurchased, leaving 8,000 - 3,000 = 5,000 incremental dilutive shares. Choice B (8,000) incorrectly ignores the treasury stock assumption, overstating the dilutive impact.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Two-class method vs treasury stock method determination
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Corp. has common stock outstanding and Series A preferred stock that participates in dividends on a pro-rata basis with common shareholders after receiving its stated dividend. The CFO is calculating EPS and must determine whether the two-class method or the treasury stock method applies to the Series A preferred. Evaluate which method is appropriate and why.', '["The treasury stock method applies because the preferred stock receives a stated dividend before participating", "The two-class method applies because the Series A preferred is a participating security that shares in undistributed earnings", "The treasury stock method applies because the preferred stock is convertible into common shares", "Neither method applies because participating preferred stock is excluded from EPS calculations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-59A, the two-class method is required when an entity has participating securities — those entitled to participate in undistributed earnings with common stock. Crestview''s Series A preferred participates pro-rata after its stated dividend, making it a participating security. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method applies to stock options and warrants, not to participating securities that share in earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the term antidilutive mean in the context of earnings per share?', '["A security that reduces the number of shares outstanding", "A security excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS", "A security whose inclusion would increase EPS or decrease loss per share", "A security that has no effect on the EPS calculation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, antidilutive refers to an increase in EPS or a decrease in loss per share resulting from including a potentially dilutive security. Antidilutive securities must be excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would make EPS appear more favorable than basic EPS. Choice B is incorrect because antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS entirely, not included in it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — All securities antidilutive in net loss scenario
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries reports a net loss of $320,000 with 100,000 weighted-average shares. The company has 10,000 convertible preferred shares (each converting into 2 common shares, $30,000 annual dividend) and $200,000 of 5% convertible bonds (converting into 8,000 shares, tax rate 25%). The assistant controller wants to include the convertible preferred in diluted EPS because its per-share impact of $1.50 appears dilutive. Evaluate this reasoning.', '["The reasoning is flawed because all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive when the entity reports a net loss, and diluted EPS equals basic EPS", "The reasoning is correct because the per-share impact is positive, reducing loss per share in a beneficial way", "The reasoning is flawed because only stock options are antidilutive during a net loss, not convertible securities", "The reasoning is correct because ASC 260 requires all convertible securities in diluted EPS regardless of profitability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, when an entity reports a net loss from continuing operations, inclusion of any potentially dilutive securities would decrease the loss per share, which is antidilutive. Therefore all potentially dilutive securities must be excluded, and diluted EPS equals basic EPS. Choice B incorrectly concludes that reducing the loss per share is dilutive — ASC 260 treats any improvement in per-share results as antidilutive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Participating securities in basic EPS allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redfield Corp. has 200,000 common shares and 50,000 participating preferred shares that share in undistributed earnings equally with common stock on a per-share basis. Net income is $750,000, and preferred dividends of $100,000 are paid. The controller calculates basic EPS as ($750,000 - $100,000) / 200,000 = $3.25. Analyze whether this calculation complies with ASC 260.', '["The calculation complies because preferred dividends were properly deducted before dividing by common shares", "The calculation does not comply because the preferred dividend should not be deducted since the stock participates", "The calculation complies but the denominator should include preferred shares, giving EPS of $2.60", "The calculation does not comply because the two-class method requires allocating undistributed earnings, resulting in basic EPS of $2.60"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-59A, the two-class method is required when participating securities exist. Undistributed earnings ($750,000 - $100,000 = $650,000) must be allocated between common (200,000) and participating preferred (50,000) on a per-share basis: common receives $650,000 x (200,000/250,000) = $520,000, yielding basic EPS of $2.60. Choice A incorrectly applies the standard formula and ignores the two-class allocation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares — conditions NOT met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonebridge Inc. agreed to issue 20,000 additional shares to selling shareholders if cumulative revenue exceeds $5 million within two years of an acquisition. At the end of Year 1, cumulative revenue is $3.8 million. Analyze how these contingently issuable shares affect Year 1 basic EPS.', '["Include all 20,000 shares in the weighted-average for the full year because the target will likely be met", "Include 20,000 shares weighted from the acquisition date because issuance is probable", "Exclude the shares from basic EPS because the revenue condition has not been satisfied at the reporting date", "Include 15,200 shares based on the pro-rata proportion of the revenue target achieved"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all necessary conditions have been satisfied at the end of the reporting period. Since Stonebridge''s cumulative revenue of $3.8 million has not reached the $5 million threshold, the 20,000 shares are excluded from basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because likelihood of future achievement does not meet ASC 260''s requirement that conditions must be satisfied by period-end.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Two-class method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the two-class method of computing earnings per share?', '["A method that presents EPS for both common stock and preferred stock on separate lines", "A method that allocates earnings between common stock and participating securities based on their dividend rights", "A method that computes separate EPS figures for basic and diluted presentations", "A method that requires computing EPS using both the treasury stock and if-converted methods simultaneously"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60, the two-class method is an earnings allocation formula that determines EPS for each class of common stock and participating security based on dividends declared and participation rights in undistributed earnings. It applies when an entity has participating securities. Choice A is incorrect because the two-class method allocates earnings to participating securities rather than simply presenting separate line items.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Warrants treasury stock method computation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Broadstone Corp. has 150,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding and net income of $480,000 with no preferred stock. The company has 12,000 outstanding warrants with an exercise price of $25 per share. The average market price during the year was $50. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.08", "$3.20", "$2.96", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised at the beginning of the period with proceeds used to repurchase shares at the average market price. Broadstone''s 12,000 warrants generate $300,000 in proceeds ($25 x 12,000), repurchasing 6,000 shares ($300,000 / $50), producing 6,000 incremental shares. Diluted EPS = $480,000 / 156,000 = $3.08. Choice C ($2.96) incorrectly adds all 12,000 shares without the treasury stock offset.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Per-share amount for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fletcher Industries reports net income of $250,000, which includes a loss from discontinued operations of $80,000. The company has 50,000 weighted-average common shares with no preferred stock. What is the per-share amount for the loss from discontinued operations?', '["$(5.00)", "$(3.40)", "$(2.50)", "$(1.60)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, per-share amounts for discontinued operations are computed by dividing the discontinued operations amount by the weighted-average shares. Fletcher''s loss from discontinued operations: $80,000 / 50,000 shares = $(1.60) per share. Choice A ($(5.00)) incorrectly divides the full net income by the shares rather than isolating the discontinued operations component.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Actual bond conversion effect on basic vs diluted denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Corp. has $400,000 of 10% convertible bonds that were converted into 20,000 common shares on April 1. Before conversion, 80,000 common shares were outstanding. The tax rate is 25%. Analyze the effect on the denominator for both basic and diluted EPS for the full year.', '["Basic EPS uses 100,000 shares; diluted EPS uses 100,000 shares", "Basic EPS uses 95,000 shares; diluted EPS uses 100,000 shares", "Basic EPS uses 80,000 shares; diluted EPS uses 100,000 shares", "Basic EPS uses 95,000 shares; diluted EPS uses 95,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible bonds are actually converted, basic EPS weights the new shares from the conversion date (80,000 x 3/12 + 100,000 x 9/12 = 95,000). For diluted EPS, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the period start, including all 20,000 bond shares for the full year (denominator = 100,000). Choice C incorrectly excludes the converted shares from the basic EPS denominator entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred in EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halstead Corp. has $500,000 of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock that pays a 6% annual dividend and must be redeemed in five years. The company also has 100,000 common shares and net income of $380,000. Analyze how the mandatorily redeemable preferred stock dividends affect the basic EPS numerator.', '["No deduction is required because mandatorily redeemable preferred is classified as a liability and its dividends are already reflected as interest expense in net income", "Deduct $30,000 because all preferred stock dividends reduce the basic EPS numerator", "Deduct $30,000 plus any redemption premium because the stock has a mandatory redemption feature", "Deduct only the portion exceeding the market rate of interest because the remainder is a liability cost"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability rather than equity. Payments to holders are treated as interest expense, already deducted in arriving at net income. Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only dividends on equity-classified preferred stock are deducted from the EPS numerator. Choice B is incorrect because deducting $30,000 again would create a double deduction, understating basic EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Beacon Corp. has written put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 of its own common shares at $60 per share. The average market price during the period is $45 per share. Net income is $500,000 with 200,000 weighted-average shares and no preferred dividends. Analyze the effect of these written put options on diluted EPS.', '["The put options are antidilutive and excluded because the exercise price exceeds the market price", "The put options add 10,000 shares to the denominator, reducing diluted EPS to $2.38", "The put options add approximately 3,333 incremental shares under the reverse treasury stock method, reducing diluted EPS", "The put options reduce the denominator by 10,000 shares because the entity must repurchase its own stock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity issues shares at the $45 market price to generate the $600,000 needed to settle the puts (13,333 shares), then retires 10,000 shares, creating approximately 3,333 incremental dilutive shares. Choice A is incorrect because written put options are dilutive (not antidilutive) when exercise price exceeds market price.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — What is deducted from numerator for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which amount is deducted from net income in the numerator of the basic EPS formula?', '["Interest expense on convertible bonds", "Dividends paid to common shareholders", "Unrealized gains included in other comprehensive income", "Preferred stock dividends for the period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, the numerator for basic EPS is net income reduced by preferred stock dividends — whether declared (for noncumulative) or accumulated (for cumulative). This adjustment isolates earnings available to common shareholders. Choice A is incorrect because interest on convertible bonds is added back to the numerator only when computing diluted EPS under the if-converted method, not deducted for basic EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Shares from business combination mid-year weighting
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On September 1, Grayson Corp. acquired Hillside Inc. by issuing 40,000 common shares. Before the acquisition, Grayson had 160,000 common shares outstanding all year. For basic EPS purposes, what is Grayson''s weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for the year?', '["200,000", "173,333", "180,000", "160,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. Grayson''s 40,000 acquisition shares are weighted for 4 months (September through December): 160,000 + (40,000 x 4/12) = 160,000 + 13,333 = 173,333. Choice A (200,000) incorrectly includes all acquisition shares for the full year, overstating the denominator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Stock dividend declared after balance sheet date but before issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornton Corp. has 100,000 common shares outstanding as of December 31, Year 1. On February 15, Year 2 — before the Year 1 financial statements are issued — the board declares a 20% stock dividend. Analyze how this post-period stock dividend affects the Year 1 weighted-average shares for EPS.', '["The weighted-average shares for Year 1 must be retroactively adjusted to 120,000 because stock dividends declared before issuance receive retroactive treatment", "The weighted-average shares remain at 100,000 because the stock dividend is a Year 2 event", "The weighted-average shares are adjusted to 110,000, reflecting a partial-year weighting of the stock dividend", "The stock dividend is disclosed in the notes but does not affect the Year 1 EPS computation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, a stock dividend occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance must be given retroactive effect in computing EPS for all periods presented. Thornton''s 20% stock dividend adjusts the Year 1 weighted-average shares from 100,000 to 120,000. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 specifically requires retroactive treatment for stock dividends regardless of when they are declared relative to the period end.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Basic EPS with multiple share issuances throughout the year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Corp. had 90,000 shares outstanding on January 1. It issued 30,000 shares on April 1 and another 20,000 shares on October 1. Net income for the year was $470,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.36", "$3.92", "$4.00", "$5.22"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued at different dates are time-weighted. Kensington: 90,000 x 3/12 = 22,500; 120,000 x 6/12 = 60,000; 140,000 x 3/12 = 35,000. Total weighted-average = 117,500. Basic EPS = $470,000 / 117,500 = $4.00. Choice A ($3.36) incorrectly uses the ending share count of 140,000 for the full year, ignoring the time-weighting requirement for shares issued during the period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Sequential inclusion test showing antidilutive second security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Evergreen Corp. reports net income of $1,000,000 with 250,000 weighted-average shares and $50,000 in preferred dividends (basic EPS = $3.80). The company has options producing 12,000 incremental shares and $1,000,000 of 15% convertible bonds (tax rate 25%, convertible into 25,000 shares). Analyze whether both securities should be included in diluted EPS.', '["$3.70 — both securities are included because each has a positive per-share impact", "$3.80 — both securities are antidilutive because the bonds'' impact exceeds basic EPS", "$3.63 — only the options are included because the bonds are also dilutive when tested", "$3.63 — only the options are included because the bonds'' per-share impact of $4.50 exceeds the tentative diluted EPS, making them antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested sequentially from most to least dilutive. Options (impact $0/12,000 shares) are included first, reducing EPS to $950,000/262,000 = $3.63. The bonds'' per-share impact is $112,500 (after-tax interest) / 25,000 shares = $4.50, which exceeds $3.63 — making them antidilutive. Only options are included. Choice A incorrectly includes both, which would increase EPS to $3.70.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred dividend add-back in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. has net income of $600,000, 150,000 weighted-average common shares, and 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock convertible into 30,000 common shares. Basic EPS is $3.73 after deducting $40,000 in preferred dividends. The conversion is dilutive. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.11", "$3.33", "$3.73", "$3.53"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back the $40,000 preferred dividends to the numerator (giving $600,000) and adds the 30,000 conversion shares to the denominator (giving 180,000). Diluted EPS = $600,000 / 180,000 = $3.33. Choice A ($3.11) incorrectly fails to add back the preferred dividends, double-counting the deduction and understating the EPS numerator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluate stock split retroactive vs prospective treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplewood Holdings had 100,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, it completed a 2-for-1 stock split. On July 1, it issued 20,000 new shares for cash. An analyst calculates weighted-average shares as 185,000. Evaluate whether the analyst''s computation is correct and determine the proper weighted-average shares under ASC 260.', '["The analyst is correct at 185,000 because the stock split is weighted from April 1", "The analyst is incorrect; the correct amount is 200,000 because only the split matters", "The analyst is incorrect; the correct amount is 210,000 because the split must be applied retroactively to the beginning of the year", "The analyst is incorrect; the correct amount is 220,000 because both the split and issuance receive retroactive treatment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits receive retroactive treatment as if occurring at the beginning of the earliest period presented. The pre-split 100,000 shares become 200,000 retroactively. Weighted average: 200,000 for 12 months plus 20,000 x 6/12 for the July issuance = 210,000. The analyst''s error (185,000) came from weighting the split prospectively from April 1. Choice D is incorrect because share issuances for cash are weighted from the issuance date, not retroactively.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
